WEBVTT

cue-VID_U6_01-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Meet Brambledon Bicycles, a mid sized maker of city bikes. A glowing loop turns through growth, boom, recession and slump. Arrows advance around the ring. Watch arrows pass four gates, one for each stage. Each stage shifts demand, output, employment and the goals this firm can pursue.

cue-VID_U6_01-2
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:48.000
During growth, orders rise and Brambledon adds shifts. In the boom, demand is strong but wages climb as workers become scarce. Recession brings falling orders and spare capacity. Slump means deep cuts, job losses and a fight for cash. Notice how demand, output and employment all move together around the loop every time. The arrow then returns to growth, so the pattern repeats again and again.

cue-VID_U6_01-3
00:00:48.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Inflation means the average price level of goods and services rises over time. An index tile climbs from one hundred to one hundred five, so a hundred dollars buys less. Employment supports demand, but tight labour pushes wages up. Unemployment means willing workers cannot find jobs, weakening demand for bikes. Higher material costs squeeze margin unless Brambledon raises prices, which may cost sales in a competitive market.

cue-VID_U6_01-4
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Gross domestic product, or GDP, is the total value of output of goods and services in a country in one year. Economic growth is when real GDP increases. A recession is falling GDP, usually two consecutive quarters of negative growth. Brambledon watches these figures before planning stock. If GDP falls for two quarters, the firm delays its new frame line.

cue-VID_U6_01-5
00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Fiscal policy uses taxation and public spending to influence demand. Corporation tax comes out of retained profit, leaving less for Brambledon to invest. Higher income tax cuts disposable income, so customers buy fewer bikes. More government spending can create orders, or compete for scarce resources and push costs up. A lower tax rate leaves more cash, but the government then collects less to spend on services.

cue-VID_U6_01-6
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Monetary policy works through interest rates. Brambledon's loan rate rises from three percent to seven percent. Because borrowing costs rise, monthly repayments take more profit, so the firm postpones a new workshop. Customers face dearer credit too, and consumer spending falls. Lower rates would reverse both effects. The transmission runs from the rate, to the loan, to spending, then to orders and to jobs across the supply chain.

cue-VID_U6_01-7
00:02:36.000 --> 00:03:08.000
On one side, lower rates and lower taxes lift demand and profit. Brambledon can hire and expand. On the other side, cheap credit can fuel inflation, and rate rises make loans expensive. Higher taxes reduce profit but fund services that support demand. Which lever matters most depends on whether the problem is weak sales or rising costs. A boom needs cooling, while a slump needs stimulus, so policy must match the stage of the cycle we are in.

cue-VID_U6_01-8
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:44.000
During a downturn Brambledon acts. Because orders fall and cash is tight, therefore it cuts overtime and delays expansion, so it protects cash. It changes product mix toward cheaper commuter bikes, negotiates supplier terms and targets students. Cutting costs preserves the firm, but deep cuts can damage quality and morale. A firm that keeps training may recover faster when growth returns. Stock is reduced, prices are held where possible, and new models are postponed until demand improves. The lesson is to match spending to the stage of the cycle.

cue-VID_U6_01-9
00:03:44.000 --> 00:04:16.000
Time to apply this. Imagine interest rates rise and income tax falls at the same time. Which effect hits Brambledon first, and which is more significant for a firm with a large loan? On screen, two levers move in opposite directions. Choose your answer, then watch the arrows show the result. A larger loan means heavier repayments, so the rate rise usually bites harder than extra spending power from tax in the short run for this business.

